Vocab/nature/concept/Hint: Idea explained for its own sake
Vocab/nature/ref/Hint: Facts you come back to look up
Vocab/nature/guide/Hint: How-to, step by step
Vocab/nature/pattern/Hint: Shape that solves a recurring problem
Vocab/nature/snippet/Hint: Fragment to copy as is
Vocab/nature/template/Hint: Starting point to duplicate
Vocab/nature/decision/Hint: Settled choice, and why
Vocab/nature/log/Hint: What happened, dated
Vocab/nature/hub/Hint: Way in to other tiddlers
Vocab/nature/task/Hint: One thing to do
Vocab/nature/tasklist/Hint: Tasks tracked together
Vocab/nature/routine/Hint: Done again and again, on a rhythm
Vocab/status/archived/Hint: Kept for the record, unmaintained
Vocab/status/deprecated/Hint: Superseded — do not rely on it
Vocab/status/stable/Hint: Reviewed, reliable, safe to build on
Vocab/status/raw/Hint: Dumped quickly, unreviewed

Vocabularies/Caption: Vocabularies
Vocabularies/Intro: The `nature` and `status` fields take their value from a fixed list. What the field holds is the //stored value//; what you read in the editor and in your lists is its label in the wiki's language. The two are kept apart on purpose, so that filters go on working whichever language the wiki is set to.
Vocabularies/Column/Value: Value
Vocabularies/Column/Stored: Stored in the field
Vocabularies/Column/Meaning: Means
Vocabularies/Outro: Write filters against the stored value: `[nature[guide]]`, `[status[stable]]`. To show a value to a reader, use `<$transclude $variable="bf-vocab-item" field="nature" value={{!!nature}}/>`, which renders its icon and its label in the current language.
